There's a couple of poems that belong to other folks, and then there is an extension of what looks like, otherwise looks like right-of-way.

250
00:18:36,380 --> 00:18:36,920
It's not.

251
00:18:37,040 --> 00:18:38,660
It was never platted as right-of-way.

252
00:18:38,720 --> 00:18:39,900
It just looks like that.

253
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,420
It was never a dedication, public dedication.

254
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:44,820
It goes back out to the east of where the whole.

255
00:18:44,820 --> 00:18:54,940
That piece of land is part of the subject property, and is intended to become part of the open space.

256
00:18:55,060 --> 00:19:01,680
Nothing will be built there, and it won't be relied upon in any way to support construction that's part of the subject.

257
00:19:03,160 --> 00:19:05,200
Or the condominium, as it is.

258
00:19:05,660 --> 00:19:07,520
It's a zero-lot-lot condominium.

259
00:19:07,520 --> 00:19:27,700
That said, the houses that abut that 50-foot parcel of land through the east and the west each have their driveway access to Little Mill Road within the frontage of that strip of land.

260
00:19:27,700 --> 00:19:49,940
That was intentional, because when those two houses were built, they came out of the same piece of property that this is a residual of, and there was contemplation of, this is well over a decade ago, of continuing the subdivision one day and building a road.

261
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:51,020
And it never happened.

262
00:19:51,020 --> 00:20:15,680
The reason I raised this is where this is going to become open space in the condominium, if there is not existing easement rights over that land for the benefit of those two homeowners, I would want to see them granted at this time so that we don't end up with an encumbrance on the open space.

263
00:20:15,680 --> 00:20:25,360
If there is a title conflict, that could effectively and inadvertently affect three parties, you know, the two existing homeowners.

264
00:20:25,960 --> 00:20:30,420
You can just get an easement there from Mr. Story and take care of that.

265
00:20:31,180 --> 00:20:31,260
Yep.

266
00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:31,820
All right.

267
00:20:31,900 --> 00:20:45,660
Under zoning matters, really one of the things that, you know, if you look at the plan, you know, this is a fairly good representation.

268
00:20:45,680 --> 00:20:59,900
of one of the choices that we intentionally put options in, we built the open space development ordinance about 15 years ago that we wanted to see.

269
00:20:59,900 --> 00:21:20,460
And part of one of the reasons that we did this at that time was there was a fledgling piece of statute out there that was just been adopted and signed into law that was originally going to take effect in 2009.

270
00:21:20,460 --> 00:21:27,040
It was delayed effective date to 2010, and that is the workforce housing statute, right?

271
00:21:27,900 --> 00:21:49,220
So by this zero lot line land condominium is really what this is, compresses the available residential density to a small portion of the premises.

272
00:21:50,460 --> 00:22:04,880
In this particular case, that has secondary benefit because of proximity of the land on the southerly portion of the property, which is the majority of it, to Shoal Pond.

273
00:22:04,880 --> 00:22:14,400
I mean, effectively, this creates conservation land adjacent to Shoal Pond that will be preserved in that fashion in perpetuity, right?

274
00:22:14,400 --> 00:22:28,140
But the, one of the requirements for the open space development ordinance is a 50 foot perimeter buffer.

275
00:22:28,140 --> 00:22:41,100
Now, remember, out front, there's the lots that came out of the parent parcel at, you know, decades past that have existing homes on them.

276
00:22:41,260 --> 00:22:49,520
Around the periphery of those where they share a common boundary with the development track, there's a 50 foot natural buffer.

277
00:22:49,520 --> 00:23:00,420
And under the language of the ordinance, that perimeter buffer shall be comprised of existing and planted vegetation.

278
00:23:00,420 --> 00:23:07,260
And as my remark continues, the italics is taken directly from the ordinance.

279
00:23:07,260 --> 00:23:26,420
In the case of an open space development with a zero lot line layout arrangement, no buildings, pavement, above ground utility installations, or other permanent improvements shall be situated within any portion of the required perimeter landscape buffer unless so approved by the planning board.

280
00:23:26,420 --> 00:23:52,920
So, it's basically the setup to make sure that we had a viable buffer, however, as a safety net that our board can use your, you know, authority under innovative zoning, this, that open space development ordinance was adopted under authority of RSA 67421 as an innovative zoning technique.

281
00:23:52,920 --> 00:24:07,060
So, rather than running off to the ZBA to get some variance for some aspect of it that's tied to the OSD ordinance, your board has the authority to grant dispensation if you feel appropriate.

282
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:16,000
In this particular case, if you take a look at the plans in a careful way, you'll notice that there's graded embankment.

283
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:37,920
Some of the building sites, again, this is effectively a land condominium, adjoin the natural buffer, and there's grading that goes into the buffer, and that's pretty easy to replant.

284
00:24:37,920 --> 00:24:49,540
And there's also half a dozen private wells that are contemplated being installed within that buffer.

285
00:24:49,760 --> 00:24:59,380
So, at some point, you know, probably not tonight, but, you know, I'm going to suggest that you take a pretty good look at the plans and have Ryan give you a detailed explanation

286
00:24:59,380 --> 00:25:10,400
of the extent of the extent of activity that they would be providing or proposing within the buffer, and what mitigation for that would be pro-offered, right?

287
00:25:11,540 --> 00:25:16,460
Obviously, grading of embankment, not a big deal.

288
00:25:18,200 --> 00:25:24,000
Bring it to a line and grade that is probably superior to what's there today and do a planting plan.

289
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:29,960
And the question is, from the Board's perspective, is how robust does that want to be, in my opinion?

290
00:25:30,680 --> 00:25:42,820
And then, obviously, there is going to have to be incursion into that buffer, not only in the initial instance, to install any drilled wells, but also potentially to maintain the well heads at some point.

291
00:25:43,480 --> 00:25:53,400
So, those are things that would need to be thought about before that dispensation that they're going to need can be given.

292
00:25:54,000 --> 00:26:01,900
So, continuing at the top of page three with zoning matters, I'm going to skip down to the third item.

293
00:26:04,420 --> 00:26:09,800
Again, it comes back to the zero lot line layout and condominium form of ownership.

294
00:26:10,500 --> 00:26:19,940
As Ryan indicated, draft copies of those documents have been submitted, presumably, and we need to chase those down.

295
00:26:19,940 --> 00:26:28,900
That always seems like a formality to people, but in the case of condominiums, with maintenance of open space, it's not.

296
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:29,940
It's not at all.

297
00:26:30,060 --> 00:26:35,700
There's a lot of provisions in there that, you know, like we saw at the villas, right, recently,

298
00:26:35,700 --> 00:26:44,920
where there was some special provisions in there that ended up with the town of Sandown getting fee simple title,

299
00:26:45,040 --> 00:26:48,920
the seven acres of land that was very important to the Conservation Commission, right?

300
00:26:48,920 --> 00:26:56,100
So, on this, as far as the protection of this open space and management of this open space,

301
00:26:56,180 --> 00:27:02,740
I suspect that will be one of the conversations Ryan may have with the Conservation Commission upcoming.

302
00:27:02,920 --> 00:27:08,940
So, we want to keep that a little fluid until everybody that has opportunity to weigh in, we've heard from, right?

303
00:27:08,940 --> 00:27:17,120
So, the fourth item is more of a technical item.

304
00:27:17,640 --> 00:27:19,620
We need a few more test bits, that type of thing.

305
00:27:21,020 --> 00:27:27,760
And the fifth item on zoning matters is the conditional use permit.

306
00:27:27,760 --> 00:27:42,860
Obviously, there is a proposal to impact 1,645 square feet, and Ryan will be taking that up, I understand,

307
00:27:42,940 --> 00:27:47,360
with the Conservation Commission in a couple of weeks, and they'll weigh in and get back to us with that.

308
00:27:47,360 --> 00:28:00,160
And so, until we have that happy letter from the Conservation Commission, that's going to stay on the sideline, I guess,

309
00:28:00,280 --> 00:28:01,620
would be your custom, right?

310
00:28:01,920 --> 00:28:02,180
Okay.

311
00:28:03,220 --> 00:28:07,660
Under planning and design matters, I'm not going to talk about many of these,

312
00:28:07,760 --> 00:28:14,520
because a lot of these are just technical items that Ryan and his consulting engineer, I'm certain, will take care of.

313
00:28:14,520 --> 00:28:23,960
But, on page 4, item 4, there is a waiver request here under Section 9.23,

314
00:28:24,820 --> 00:28:30,780
customary request for waiver of on-site recreational opportunities,

315
00:28:31,360 --> 00:28:36,700
and Ryan can speak to that, because he has a proposal for an alternative means of providing that,

316
00:28:36,780 --> 00:28:39,340
that he'll present at the appropriate time.

317
00:28:39,340 --> 00:28:48,940
The fifth remark, when the application first came in, similar to the prior application,

318
00:28:49,100 --> 00:28:55,140
it presumed that the total length of Dead End Street was going to exceed 1,000 feet.

319
00:28:57,440 --> 00:29:04,340
In my memo of June 16th, I questioned that, because I didn't think it was,

320
00:29:04,340 --> 00:29:13,540
and they came back and demonstrated that it's 976.61 feet, so that waiver request can be withdrawn.

321
00:29:14,100 --> 00:29:14,240
Okay.

322
00:29:14,800 --> 00:29:21,640
As a practical matter, after, I forget what the effective date is, in 2027,

323
00:29:22,640 --> 00:29:29,660
you're not going to, municipalities will no longer be able to have maximum dead-end lengths.

324
00:29:29,660 --> 00:29:35,900
It will all come down to the, as part of their planning and zoning ordinances,

325
00:29:36,200 --> 00:29:51,000
they will default to NFPA and be controlled locally by the authority having just jurisdiction to administer NFPA 1.

326
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:56,120
So, that's just kind of some news.

327
00:29:59,700 --> 00:30:00,860
All right.

328
00:30:01,220 --> 00:30:03,480
And then, other items.

329
00:30:05,700 --> 00:30:08,300
This may be one of, somewhere in there.

330
00:30:09,740 --> 00:30:11,460
I know I mentioned it.

331
00:30:11,460 --> 00:30:19,200
If you look closely at the plans, there's, I mentioned some, there's some water wells

332
00:30:19,200 --> 00:30:25,480
that are proposed that are within the natural perimeter buffer.

333
00:30:26,200 --> 00:30:32,340
That doesn't really concern me, because you want to protect wellheads,

334
00:30:32,420 --> 00:30:35,420
and where else better to protect it than protect the plant, right?

335
00:30:35,420 --> 00:30:47,740
What does concern me is the fact that the protective radii extend onto the land significantly in some places of others.

336
00:30:47,740 --> 00:31:01,240
So, I'm thinking about this in a way that, you know, if the only land available for those wells

337
00:31:01,240 --> 00:31:09,200
was, you know, what's shown in the location where they want to be, where they're supposed to be,

338
00:31:09,200 --> 00:31:18,500
I'd probably feel a little bit different about it, but whereas, there's such an abundance of open space here, interior,

339
00:31:19,180 --> 00:31:26,540
I don't know why we would want to have protective radii fall on the land above, right?

340
00:31:27,160 --> 00:31:29,420
Now, we don't control that here.

341
00:31:29,800 --> 00:31:31,400
Now, that's not, that's a statement.

342
00:31:31,400 --> 00:31:40,980
But, I suspect that Ryan may have conversation with the DES about that.

343
00:31:42,120 --> 00:31:46,520
Because this is a condominium, it can remain one piece of land.

344
00:31:46,740 --> 00:31:48,780
It has 21 limited common areas.

345
00:31:49,320 --> 00:31:53,140
Now, basically, the difference between a land condominium and a typical condominium

346
00:31:53,140 --> 00:31:55,600
is the unit is not the home.

347
00:31:55,720 --> 00:32:02,120
The unit is the postage stamp envelope that the home is available to be built in.

348
00:32:03,740 --> 00:32:14,940
So, what I'm anticipating is that they will consider the 21 units subject to a community water system.

349
00:32:14,940 --> 00:32:21,700
You can have as many wells as you want, but they've got to play by different rules

350
00:32:21,700 --> 00:32:27,040
that would foreclose the ability to have protective radii on somebody else's property.

351
00:32:27,520 --> 00:32:29,360
So, that's something to stay tuned for.

352
00:32:29,720 --> 00:32:36,340
But, I would ask you to see if there's a way where you can do something different

353
00:32:36,340 --> 00:32:43,920
so that we're not having those well radii overlap and significantly encroach on somebody else's property.

354
00:32:44,940 --> 00:32:46,700
I just don't like that.

355
00:32:46,940 --> 00:32:54,140
I don't have something I can pick up Section X, Article Y of a local ordinance and say you can't do that.

356
00:32:54,680 --> 00:32:55,800
I just don't like it.

357
00:32:56,360 --> 00:32:57,240
So, do me a favor.

358
00:32:57,780 --> 00:32:58,580
I hear you.

359
00:33:01,400 --> 00:33:05,560
I was going to ask why all the wells have to be in that 50-foot buffer.

360
00:33:05,560 --> 00:33:10,780
So, the intent with this is that, yes, it's a land condominium,

361
00:33:11,020 --> 00:33:19,980
but the owners are very kind of strict to the point where they want the utilities to be unit-specific.

362
00:33:20,440 --> 00:33:24,280
No sharing wells, no community water system, no sharing septics.

363
00:33:24,400 --> 00:33:31,220
They wanted a well per unit, just like it's not normal single-family dwelling, typical lots,

364
00:33:31,220 --> 00:33:33,700
but that's what they want it to feel like.

365
00:33:35,220 --> 00:33:41,620
And, in doing so, just trying to keep the well radiuses away from the septic systems

366
00:33:41,620 --> 00:33:46,000
when I have such small limited common areas for each of these units to play with

367
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:52,880
creates a bit of a back-and-forth game that I'm sure I'll continue to have with New Hampshire DES

368
00:33:52,880 --> 00:33:56,000
when they get me their first round of comments on their RFMI.

369
00:33:57,260 --> 00:34:00,700
I am completely confident that you will work it out.

370
00:34:01,180 --> 00:34:04,960
I'm just not sure it's going to work out the way that you might want it to.

371
00:34:05,660 --> 00:34:06,640
Yes, that's all.

372
00:34:06,640 --> 00:34:07,560
We'll see what they say.

373
00:34:10,020 --> 00:34:11,320
I did one of these.

374
00:34:11,520 --> 00:34:14,460
My office did one in Bethlehem a few years ago.

375
00:34:14,580 --> 00:34:15,800
I know the one that you're talking about.

376
00:34:15,880 --> 00:34:19,600
Yeah, and we were told that's the last one we're ever going to get approved like this.

377
00:34:19,600 --> 00:34:24,980
So, yeah, I would be doing the same thing as you are if I was designing this for David.

378
00:34:25,400 --> 00:34:25,700
Yes.

379
00:34:27,700 --> 00:34:29,760
Is that everything with your letter?

